ight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ECrystal%20Pulling%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%22552.8%22%20y1%3D%22157.5%22%20x2%3D%22561.0%22%20y2%3D%22157.5%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%222%22%20marker-end%3D%22url%28%23arrow%29%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22565.0%22%20y%3D%22120%22%20width%3D%22115%22%20height%3D%2275%22%20fill%3D%22%23eff6ff%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.5%22%20rx%3D%226%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22585.0%22%20cy%3D%22140%22%20r%3D%2211%22%20fill%3D%22%233b82f6%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22585.0%22%20y%3D%22144%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3E5%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22622.5%22%20y%3D%22164%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EWafer%20Slicing%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E', 'Stage 1: Quartz Reduction; Stage 2: Polysilicon Refining; Stage 3: Crucible Melting; Stage 4: Crystal Pulling; Stage 5: Wafer Slicing.', 'The diagram displays the manufacturing sequence for producing monocrystalline solar silicon wafers. Quartz sand is reduced in an electric arc furnace to obtain metallurgical silicon, which is chemically refined into hyper-pure polysilicon. The refined material is melted inside a quartz crucible where the Czochralski process pulls a cylindrical monocrystalline silicon ingot. Finally, precision diamond wire saws slice the ingot into ultra-thin solar wafers. To summarize, complex thermal and chemical refining converts raw quartz into solar semiconductors.', 25, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe quartz reduction, polysilicon chemical purification, crystal growth, and diamond wire slicing.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Describe Image'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-di-050', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-describe-image', 'Describe Image', 'Municipal Effluent Tertiary Treatment and Nutrient Removal', 'Look at the image below. In 25 seconds, please speak into the microphone and describe in detail what the image is showing. You will have 40 seconds to give your response.', NULL, NULL, 'data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22720%22%20height%3D%22280%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20720%20280%22%20style%3D%22background%3A%23ffffff%3Bfont-family%3AArial%2Csans-serif%3B%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%22720%22%20height%3D%22280%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2240%22%20y%3D%2236%22%20font-size%3D%2216%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%3EEffluent%20Tertiary%20Treatment%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cdefs%3E%3Cmarker%20id%3D%22arrow%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%2010%2010%22%20refX%3D%226%22%20refY%3D%225%22%20markerWidth%3D%226%22%20markerHeight%3D%226%22%20orient%3D%22auto-start-reverse%22%3E%3Cpath%20d%3D%22M%200%201%20L%2010%205%20L%200%209%20z%22%20fill%3D%22%233b82f6%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fmarker%3E%3C%2Fdefs%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2240.0%22%20y%3D%22120%22%20width%3D%22115%22%20height%3D%2275%22%20fill%3D%22%23eff6ff%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.5%22%20rx%3D%226%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%2260.0%22%20cy%3D%22140%22%20r%3D%2211%22%20fill%3D%22%233b82f6%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2260.0%22%20y%3D%22144%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3E1%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2297.5%22%20y%3D%22164%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ESecondary%20Effluent%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%22159.0%22%20y1%3D%22157.5%22%20x2%3D%22167.2%22%20y2%3D%22157.5%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%222%22%20marker-end%3D%22url%28%23arrow%29%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22171.2%22%20y%3D%22120%22%20width%3D%22115%22%20height%3D%2275%22%20fill%3D%22%23eff6ff%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.5%22%20rx%3D%226%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22191.2%22%20cy%3D%22140%22%20r%3D%2211%22%20fill%3D%22%233b82f6%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22191.2%22%20y%3D%22144%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3E2%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22228.8%22%20y%3D%22164%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ECoagulant%20Dosing%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%22290.2%22%20y1%3D%22157.5%22%20x2%3D%22298.5%22%20y2%3D%22157.5%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%222%22%20marker-end%3D%22url%28%23arrow%29%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22302.5%22%20y%3D%22120%22%20width%3D%22115%22%20height%3D%2275%22%20fill%3D%22%23eff6ff%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.5%22%20rx%3D%226%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22322.5%22%20cy%3D%22140%22%20r%3D%2211%22%20fill%3D%22%233b82f6%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22322.5%22%20y%3D%22144%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3E3%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22360.0%22%20y%3D%22164%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EDisc%20Filtration%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%22421.5%22%20y1%3D%22157.5%22%20x2%3D%22429.8%22%20y2%3D%22157.5%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%222%22%20marker-end%3D%22url%28%23arrow%29%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22433.8%22%20y%3D%22120%22%20width%3D%22115%22%20height%3D%2275%22%20fill%3D%22%23eff6ff%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.5%22%20rx%3D%226%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22453.8%22%20cy%3D%22140%22%20r%3D%2211%22%20fill%3D%22%233b82f6%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22453.8%22%20y%3D%22144%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3E4%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22491.2%22%20y%3D%22164%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EUV%20Disinfection%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%22552.8%22%20y1%3D%22157.5%22%20x2%3D%22561.0%22%20y2%3D%22157.5%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%222%22%20marker-end%3D%22url%28%23arrow%29%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22565.0%22%20y%3D%22120%22%20width%3D%22115%22%20height%3D%2275%22%20fill%3D%22%23eff6ff%22%20stroke%3D%22%233b82f6%22%20stroke-width%3D%221.5%22%20rx%3D%226%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22585.0%22%20cy%3D%22140%22%20r%3D%2211%22%20fill%3D%22%233b82f6%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22585.0%22%20y%3D%22144%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%23ffffff%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3E5%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22622.5%22%20y%3D%22164%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22bold%22%20fill%3D%22%231e293b%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ERiver%20Discharge%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E', 'Stage 1: Secondary Effluent; Stage 2: Coagulant Dosing; Stage 3: Disc Filtration; Stage 4: UV Disinfection; Stage 5: River Discharge.', 'The flow diagram illustrates tertiary nutrient removal at a municipal wastewater treatment facility. Clarified secondary effluent is dosed with iron coagulant to precipitate dissolved phosphorus. The mixture passes through cloth disc filters to capture microscopic flocs, followed by ultraviolet light irradiation that destroys resistant viruses and bacteria. The purified water is safely returned to the receiving river. In conclusion, tertiary treatment effectively protects aquatic ecosystems by stripping remaining nutrients and pathogens.', 25, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain secondary effluent intake, phosphorus chemical dosing, disc filtration, UV disinfection, and safe environmental discharge.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Describe Image'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-001', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How pearls form', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Today I want to explain how pearls form. A natural pearl begins when a small irritant, such as a grain of sand or a tiny parasite, gets inside the shell of an oyster or a mussel. The animal cannot remove it, so it protects itself by covering the object with layers of a material called nacre, the same smooth substance that lines the inside of its shell. Nacre is made mainly of calcium carbonate, arranged in thin layers held together by a protein. Over several years, thousands of these layers build up around the irritant, and the way the layers reflect light gives the pearl its shine. Natural pearls are extremely rare, because only a tiny proportion of oysters ever produce one. In the late nineteenth century, a Japanese businessman, Kokichi Mikimoto, developed a method of producing cultured pearls by placing a small bead inside the oyster deliberately. Today almost all pearls sold are cultured. They are real pearls made of nacre, but their formation is started by people rather than by chance, which has made them far more affordable.', NULL, NULL, 'A pearl forms when an oyster covers an irritant with layers of nacre, mainly calcium carbonate, over several years. Natural pearls are rare, so Mikimoto developed cultured pearls by inserting a bead deliberately; today most pearls are cultured, real but far cheaper.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain the irritant, the layers of nacre and why pearls shine, then contrast rare natural pearls with cultured pearls.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-002', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How icebreaker ships work', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'In polar regions and in some northern seas, ships can only move for part of the year unless the ice is cleared for them. This is the job of the icebreaker. Many people imagine that an icebreaker cuts through ice like a knife, but most of them actually work by weight. The bow, or front, of the ship is rounded and sloped, so that the ship can ride up onto the ice. The weight of the vessel then presses down and breaks the ice beneath it. The broken pieces are pushed aside or underneath the hull. To do this, icebreakers need a very strong hull, much thicker than that of an ordinary ship, and powerful engines. Some can also pump water between tanks on each side, rocking the ship to free it if it becomes stuck. The largest icebreakers are powered by nuclear reactors, which allow them to operate for long periods without refuelling. Icebreakers keep ports and shipping routes open in winter, support scientific research in the Arctic and Antarctic, and help rescue ships trapped in the ice.', NULL, NULL, 'Icebreakers mostly break ice by weight: a rounded, sloped bow rides up onto the ice and the ship''s weight breaks it. They need very strong hulls and powerful engines, and some are nuclear powered. They keep ports and routes open, support polar research and rescue trapped ships.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Correct the idea that icebreakers cut ice, describe how the bow and weight break it, mention hull strength and power, and list their uses.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-003', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Why desert nights are cold', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Visitors to hot deserts are often surprised by how cold the nights can be. During the day, temperatures in some deserts rise above forty degrees Celsius, yet after sunset they can fall by twenty degrees or more. The main reason is the lack of water vapour in the air. Water vapour is a powerful greenhouse gas: in humid places it absorbs heat given off by the ground and sends some of it back, like a blanket. Desert air is very dry, so this blanket is missing, and the heat stored in the ground during the day escapes quickly into space at night. The second reason is the absence of clouds. Clouds also trap heat near the surface, and desert skies are usually clear. Finally, the surface itself matters. Sand and dry soil heat up quickly in the sun, but they also lose their heat quickly, unlike water, which stores heat for longer. This is why coastal areas have much smaller differences between day and night temperatures than inland deserts. For plants and animals, this daily swing is one of the greatest challenges of desert life.', NULL, NULL, 'Desert nights are cold because dry air holds little water vapour, which normally traps heat, clear skies let heat escape, and sand loses heat quickly. Coastal areas have smaller day-night differences, and the large daily temperature swing is a major challenge for desert life.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Give the size of the temperature drop and the three causes: dry air, clear skies and quickly cooling sand, then compare with coastal areas.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-004', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The discovery of X-rays', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'In November 1895, the German physicist Wilhelm Röntgen was experimenting with electric currents passed through glass tubes containing very little air. Although he had covered the tube with black cardboard, he noticed that a screen coated with a chemical on the other side of his laboratory began to glow. Some invisible form of radiation was passing through the cardboard. Because he did not know what it was, he called it X-radiation, the letter X standing for something unknown. Röntgen soon discovered that these rays passed easily through soft materials such as skin and muscle but were blocked by denser materials such as bone and metal. One of his first images showed the bones of his wife''s hand, with her ring clearly visible. The discovery spread with remarkable speed. Within months, doctors were using X-rays to find broken bones and bullets in wounded soldiers. In 1901 Röntgen received the first Nobel Prize in Physics. At first, however, the dangers were not understood, and many early researchers were harmed by repeated exposure before safety rules were introduced.', NULL, NULL, 'In 1895 Röntgen noticed a screen glowing when invisible rays from his tube passed through cardboard, and called them X-rays. They pass through soft tissue but not bone or metal, so doctors quickly used them to find fractures. He won the first Nobel Prize in Physics, though early users were harmed before safety rules.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe the accidental discovery, the name, what X-rays pass through, their rapid medical use, the Nobel Prize and the early dangers.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-005', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Why elephants have large ears', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Elephants are the largest land animals, and their size creates a problem: getting rid of heat. A large body produces a great deal of heat but has relatively little skin surface compared with its volume, and elephants cannot sweat in the way humans do. Their ears provide part of the solution. An African elephant''s ears are enormous and very thin, and they contain a dense network of blood vessels close to the surface. As warm blood flows through the ears, heat passes into the surrounding air. When an elephant flaps its ears, air moves across them faster, increasing the cooling effect, and on a hot day the blood leaving the ears can be noticeably cooler than the blood entering them. This helps to explain why African elephants, which live mainly in hot, open savannah, have much larger ears than Asian elephants, which often live in shaded forests. Elephants use other methods too. They spray water or mud over their bodies, and the wrinkles in their skin hold moisture, which keeps them cool as it slowly evaporates.', NULL, NULL, 'Elephants struggle to lose heat because they are huge and cannot sweat. Their thin ears, full of blood vessels, release heat, and flapping increases cooling. African elephants in hot savannah have bigger ears than Asian forest elephants, and elephants also use water, mud and wrinkled skin to stay cool.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain the heat problem, how blood vessels and flapping cool the ears, the African and Asian comparison, and the other cooling methods.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-006', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The history of the telephone', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'In 1876 Alexander Graham Bell was granted a patent in the United States for a device that could transmit speech electrically, and a few days later he made the first successful call to his assistant in another room. The invention was controversial from the start. Several other inventors had been working on similar ideas, and Bell''s company fought hundreds of legal cases to defend its patents. Early telephones were connected in pairs, but it soon became clear that a telephone was only useful if it could reach many other people. This led to the telephone exchange, where operators connected calls by hand, plugging cables into a switchboard. Many of these operators were young women, and switchboard work became one of the first large-scale office jobs for women. Automatic exchanges, which connected calls without an operator, spread during the twentieth century. By the 1950s, long-distance calls could be dialled directly, and undersea cables linked continents. The mobile phone, introduced commercially in the 1980s, completed the change: the telephone was no longer attached to a place but carried by a person.', NULL, NULL, 'Bell patented the telephone in 1876, though rivals disputed it. Telephones became useful through exchanges where operators, often women, connected calls by hand; automatic exchanges, direct dialling and undersea cables followed, and mobile phones in the 1980s made the phone personal rather than tied to a place.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Cover Bell''s patent and the disputes, the role of exchanges and operators, later automation and cables, and the mobile phone.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-007', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How birds'' skeletons are built for flight', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'The evolutionary transition from bipedal theropod ancestors to modern flight-capable birds involved extraordinary anatomical specializations. Chief among these adaptations is the evolution of a pneumatized skeletal framework. Avian bones are hollow, honeycombed with internal air sacs linked directly to the respiratory system. This architecture drastically minimizes overall body mass while preserving high torsional stiffness and fracture resistance during strenuous flapping. Concurrently, birds evolved an enlarged, keeled sternum that anchors powerful pectoralis and supracoracoideus flight muscles. The fusion of thoracic vertebrae into a rigid notarium and the consolidation of caudal vertebrae into a pygostyle provide a stable aerodynamic fuselage. Biomechanical computer simulations demonstrate that these skeletal fusions prevent elastic energy dissipation during wing downstrokes, maximizing aerodynamic efficiency and maneuverability during sustained long-distance migratory flight. The avian wishbone, or furcula, acts as a flexible spring that flexes during wing downstrokes and recoils during upstrokes. This elastic recoil conserves metabolic energy during extended migratory flights, illustrating remarkable evolutionary biomechanical optimization.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture details skeletal adaptations enabling avian flight. Hollow, pneumatized bones reduce body weight while retaining strength. An enlarged keeled sternum provides substantial anchorage for flight muscles, while fused vertebrae create a rigid fuselage that prevents energy loss during flapping strokes.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe avian flight adaptations: pneumatized hollow bones, keeled sternum, and skeletal fusion for aerodynamic efficiency.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-008', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Soil microbes and the nitrogen cycle', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Although soil appears to be an inert collection of mineral particles and decomposing organic matter, a single gram of fertile topsoil contains billions of living microorganisms fundamental to global biogeochemical cycling. In particular, microbial communities govern the terrestrial nitrogen cycle, transforming chemically inert dinitrogen gas into bioavailable nutrients. Free-living and symbiotic nitrogen-fixing bacteria utilize the nitrogenase enzyme to convert atmospheric nitrogen into ammonium. Nitrifying bacteria subsequently oxidize ammonium into nitrites and nitrates, which plant roots absorb to synthesize essential amino acids and proteins. Simultaneously, denitrifying bacteria in waterlogged, anaerobic soils convert nitrates back into gaseous nitrogen, completing the geochemical cycle. Agronomists emphasize that maintaining balanced microbial communities through cover cropping and reduced synthetic fertilizer application prevents nitrate runoff into aquatic ecosystems and preserves natural soil fertility. Genetic sequencing of soil microbial metagenomes reveals thousands of specialized enzymatic pathways that regulate these biochemical transformations. Understanding these microscopic interactions is crucial for designing sustainable agricultural systems that minimize artificial fertilizer dependency.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker explains microbial nitrogen cycling in agricultural soils. Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert atmospheric gas into ammonium, while nitrifying microbes oxidize it into plant-available nitrates. Denitrifying bacteria complete the loop by returning nitrogen gas to the atmosphere. Preserving these microbial communities sustains natural soil fertility and reduces runoff.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Summarize the stages of the microbial nitrogen cycle (fixation, nitrification, denitrification) and agronomic soil management.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-009', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How tiny particles in the air reflect sunlight', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Atmospheric aerosols are microscopic solid particles or liquid droplets suspended in the atmosphere, originating from natural volcanic emissions as well as anthropogenic fossil fuel combustion. In climate science, sulfate aerosols exert a significant direct cooling effect by scattering incoming solar radiation back into space. Furthermore, aerosols act as cloud condensation nuclei; higher aerosol concentrations produce clouds composed of a greater number of smaller droplets, which appear brighter and reflect additional solar energy—a mechanism known as the Twomey effect. Because aerosols have atmospheric lifetimes of only a few weeks, their cooling effect is geographically concentrated near industrial regions. Sophisticated spaceborne lidar instruments are now deployed on Earth-observation satellites to profile aerosol layers vertically. These precise measurements help modelers distinguish short-term aerosol cooling effects from long-term greenhouse gas warming trajectories. Furthermore, major volcanic eruptions historically injected millions of tons of sulfur dioxide into the stratosphere, causing measurable global cooling lasting several years. Quantifying these natural aerosol events helps scientists calibrate sensitivity parameters in international climate models.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture discusses how atmospheric sulfate aerosols influence Earth''s radiation balance. Directly, aerosols scatter sunlight into space; indirectly, they increase cloud reflectivity by creating denser droplets via the Twomey effect. Because aerosols are short-lived, satellite lidar tracking is vital for accurately separating aerosol cooling from greenhouse warming.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain direct scattering and indirect cloud brightening (Twomey effect) by sulfate aerosols and satellite monitoring.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-010', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Balancing electricity on a smart grid', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Traditional electric grids were designed for unidirectional power flow, transmitting electricity generated at central thermal power stations over high-voltage lines to passive consumers. However, the modern expansion of wind and solar photovoltaic generation poses significant stability challenges for legacy grid infrastructure. Renewable generation is intermittent and weather-dependent, fluctuating rapidly throughout the day. To maintain electrical equilibrium without blackouts, utility operators are deploying smart grids. Smart grids incorporate bidirectional digital sensors, automated switches, and machine learning algorithms that monitor power quality and demand in real time. When renewable production surges, smart systems can automatically divert surplus energy into utility-scale battery storage or dynamic demand-response programs. Furthermore, vehicle-to-grid technology allows electric automobiles to feed stored battery power back during peak evening demand, stabilizing the network while maximizing clean energy utilization. Advanced smart meters deployed across residential neighborhoods enable dynamic pricing, incentivizing consumers to shift heavy appliance use to off-peak hours. This automated load shifting flattens peak demand spikes, reducing the need for carbon-intensive peaker power plants.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker explains that conventional electrical grids struggle with intermittent renewable power like wind and solar. Smart grids resolve this using bidirectional digital monitoring, automated switches, and grid-scale battery storage. By coordinating decentralized generation and demand-response programs, smart grids preserve system stability.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Contrast centralized legacy grids with modern smart grids, focusing on real-time load balancing and renewable energy storage.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-011', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How muscles grow stronger', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'When you begin a new exercise programme, you often become stronger within a few weeks, before your muscles have visibly changed. This early improvement comes mainly from the nervous system. The brain learns to activate more muscle fibres at the same time and to coordinate them more efficiently. Longer-term gains come from changes in the muscle itself. Demanding exercise, such as lifting heavy weights, causes tiny areas of damage in muscle fibres. In the hours and days afterwards, the body repairs this damage and builds new proteins, so that the fibres become thicker. This growth is called hypertrophy. For it to happen, muscles need a sufficient challenge, which is why training programmes gradually increase the weight or the number of repetitions, a principle known as progressive overload. Rest is equally important, because the rebuilding happens during recovery, not during the exercise itself, and sleep plays a significant role. Diet matters as well, particularly an adequate supply of protein. Finally, the saying ''use it or lose it'' applies: when training stops, muscle size and strength gradually decline.', NULL, NULL, 'Early strength gains come from the nervous system activating muscle more efficiently. Later, exercise damages fibres, which are rebuilt thicker, a process called hypertrophy. This needs progressive overload, rest, sleep and enough protein, and strength declines again if training stops.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Separate early nervous-system gains from later hypertrophy, and mention progressive overload, recovery, protein and loss after stopping.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-012', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Why volcanic ash is dangerous to aircraft', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Following explosive volcanic eruptions, atmospheric winds can carry expansive plumes of fine volcanic ash thousands of kilometers across international civil aviation corridors. Unlike soft wood smoke, volcanic ash consists of tiny, abrasive shards of pulverized silicate rock and volcanic glass, presenting an acute hazard to modern turbofan aircraft. When a jet engine ingests volcanic ash at high cruising altitudes, operating temperatures inside the combustion chamber—often exceeding one thousand five hundred degrees Celsius—instantly melt the silicate particles. As the molten material travels into the cooler turbine section, it solidifies onto turbine blades, choking airflow and causing engine flameouts. Additionally, abrasive ash sandblasts cockpit windshields and clogs airspeed sensors. Modern turbine coatings incorporate thermal ceramics, but strict avoidance of contaminated airspace coordinated by international satellite advisory centers remains the primary aviation safety protocol. Atmospheric dispersion models combine real-time satellite imagery with wind vector forecasts to predict the movement of ash clouds. These automated forecasts allow international air traffic control agencies to safely reroute commercial flights around contaminated airspace.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker explains the aviation dangers posed by volcanic ash plumes. Silicate particles melt within hot jet combustion chambers and solidify onto cooler turbine blades, leading to sudden engine failure. Ash also scratches cockpit windshields and disables airspeed instruments, necessitating international satellite monitoring to divert commercial flights.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe why volcanic ash is hazardous to jet turbines (melting and resolidifying silicates) and why satellite tracking is necessary.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-013', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The invention of the steam engine', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'In the early eighteenth century, the deep coal and tin mines of England faced a serious problem: they constantly filled with water. In 1712 Thomas Newcomen built a steam engine to pump the water out. Steam from a boiler filled a cylinder and was then cooled with a spray of water, creating a partial vacuum, so that air pressure pushed a piston down and worked the pump. Newcomen''s engine was reliable but very wasteful, because the cylinder was heated and cooled with every stroke. In 1769 James Watt patented a crucial improvement: a separate condenser, where the steam was cooled without cooling the main cylinder. This greatly reduced the amount of coal needed. Watt and his business partner Matthew Boulton later developed engines that produced rotary motion, which could drive machinery in factories rather than simply pumping water. Steam power no longer depended on a site beside a fast river, so factories could be built in towns. During the nineteenth century, steam engines powered railways and ships, transforming transport, trade and the growth of cities.', NULL, NULL, 'Newcomen built a steam engine in 1712 to pump water from mines, but it wasted fuel. Watt''s separate condenser of 1769 made engines far more efficient, and rotary engines let steam drive factory machinery anywhere, later powering railways and ships.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain the mining problem, Newcomen''s engine and its weakness, Watt''s condenser, rotary motion and the later impact on factories and transport.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-014', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Robots that work in swarms', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'In nature, social insect colonies coordinate complex foraging and construction tasks without any centralized leader or hierarchy. Robotics engineers are adapting these biological principles to create autonomous multi-robot swarms. Instead of building a single complex and expensive machine that represents a single point of failure, developers deploy dozens or hundreds of compact, inexpensive robotic units. These robots communicate exclusively with immediate neighbors using local infrared signals or short-range radio protocols. When deployed in search-and-rescue operations or planetary surface mapping, the collective swarm exhibits remarkable adaptability and fault tolerance. If individual units fail or are damaged, the remaining swarm automatically reconfigures to fulfill the mission. In agricultural applications, autonomous drone swarms can survey expansive crop fields simultaneously, demonstrating that decentralized local interactions can produce robust, flexible global collective intelligence. In warehouse logistics, coordinated swarms of autonomous mobile robots transport inventory shelves simultaneously without colliding. Their decentralized collision avoidance algorithms ensure smooth operational flow even when individual units stop for battery recharging.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture discusses swarm intelligence in robotics, inspired by social insect colonies that achieve collective goals without centralized command. By deploying numerous simple, low-cost robots communicating locally, engineers build systems that are adaptable and fault-tolerant, suitable for search-and-rescue and environmental monitoring.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Summarize how decentralized insect behaviors inspire resilient swarm robotics for search-and-rescue and field exploration.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-015', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Why melting sea ice speeds up warming', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'The planetary climate system is governed by an energy balance between incoming solar radiation and the portion reflected into space, quantified as surface albedo. Fresh snow and sea ice possess an exceptionally high albedo, reflecting up to eighty-five percent of incoming solar energy. In stark contrast, open ocean water has a low albedo, absorbing approximately ninety percent of solar rays as thermal heat. As rising temperatures melt polar sea ice, reflective white surfaces are replaced by dark, heat-absorbent water. This transition drives the ice-albedo positive feedback loop: increased heat absorption warms ocean surface layers, which in turn accelerates further melting of surrounding ice. Satellite altimetry confirms that Arctic summer sea ice volume has contracted by more than seventy percent over recent decades, amplifying high-latitude warming and altering global atmospheric jet stream trajectories. The loss of sea ice also exposes the underlying ocean surface to wave-driven mechanical turbulence, accelerating the thermal erosion of coastal permafrost bluffs. This compounding effect releases additional sediments and organic carbon into shallow polar seas.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker explains the ice-albedo feedback loop, contrasting the high reflectivity of snow and ice with the heat-absorbing nature of open ocean water. Melting ice exposes darker water, absorbing more solar heat and driving further ice loss. This feedback loop causes Arctic amplification, accelerating warming and disrupting mid-latitude weather patterns.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain albedo differences between ice and water, the positive feedback loop of melting, and the concept of Arctic amplification.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-016', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The psychology of habits', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Much of what we do each day, from brushing our teeth to checking our phones, is done out of habit rather than through a conscious decision. Psychologists often describe a habit as a loop with three parts: a cue that triggers the behaviour, the routine itself, and a reward that makes us more likely to repeat it. Over time, as the behaviour is repeated in the same situation, it becomes automatic and needs little thought. A popular claim says that a new habit takes twenty-one days to form, but research suggests this is far too simple. In one well-known study, it took participants an average of about sixty-six days for a new behaviour to become automatic, with very large differences between individuals and between types of behaviour. Missing a single day did not seriously damage the process. This research has practical lessons. To build a good habit, it helps to link it to a clear cue, such as exercising straight after work, and to make it easy to start. To break a bad habit, changing the cue or the environment is often more effective than relying on willpower alone.', NULL, NULL, 'A habit is a loop of cue, routine and reward that becomes automatic through repetition. Research found new habits took about sixty-six days on average, not twenty-one, and missing a day did little harm. Linking habits to clear cues helps build them, and changing the environment helps break them.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe the cue-routine-reward loop, correct the twenty-one-day claim with the research, and give the practical lessons.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-017', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How oxygen first filled the atmosphere', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Approximately two point four billion years ago, Earth underwent a monumental environmental transformation termed the Great Oxidation Event. During the planet''s first two billion years, the atmosphere was devoid of free molecular oxygen, dominated instead by methane, nitrogen, and carbon dioxide. The catalyst for change was the evolution of cyanobacteria—aquatic photosynthetic microbes capable of splitting water molecules and producing oxygen gas as a byproduct. Initially, this newly generated oxygen reacted with dissolved ferrous iron in ancient oceans, precipitating massive banded iron formations across the seafloor. Once these dissolved oceanic iron sinks were completely exhausted, oxygen accumulated in the atmosphere. The sudden atmospheric oxygen surge proved lethal to dominant anaerobic organisms, while facilitating the subsequent development of the stratospheric ozone layer and paving the evolutionary path for aerobic multicellular life. The sudden transition to an oxygenated atmosphere also precipitated the Huronian glaciation, as atmospheric methane—a potent greenhouse gas—was oxidized into less effective carbon dioxide. This planetary freeze underscores how early microbial evolution profoundly altered global climate.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker describes the Great Oxidation Event 2.4 billion years ago, initiated by cyanobacteria producing oxygen via photosynthesis. After oxygen saturated dissolved oceanic iron, creating banded iron formations, it accumulated in the atmosphere. This caused a mass extinction of anaerobic organisms while setting the stage for complex aerobic life.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Summarize how cyanobacteria produced oxygen, the saturation of iron sinks, and the resulting mass extinction and emergence of aerobic organisms.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-018', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Carbon nanotubes in aircraft materials', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Carbon nanotubes are cylindrical nanostructures composed of rolled hexagonal graphene sheets, with diameters measured in nanometers and lengths extending up to several centimeters. These microscopic tubes possess exceptional mechanical and physical attributes. Due to the immense strength of carbon-carbon covalent bonds, nanotubes exhibit tensile strength over one hundred times greater than structural steel while weighing only a fraction as much. Depending on the chiral orientation of their atomic lattice, carbon nanotubes can behave as metallic electrical conductors or as semiconductors, with thermal conductivity surpassing diamond. Engineers are incorporating carbon nanotubes into polymer composites for aerospace applications, producing ultra-lightweight aircraft fuselages and satellite components. Furthermore, their high surface area makes them promising materials for next-generation energy storage electrodes. Integrating carbon nanotubes into conductive polymer coatings also provides lightning strike protection for composite aircraft structures. This multifunctional capability eliminates the need for heavy copper mesh layers, yielding significant fuel economy advantages across modern civil commercial aviation.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture highlights carbon nanotubes, cylindrical nanostructures of rolled graphene known for immense tensile strength and versatility. Possessing high electrical and thermal conductivity, nanotubes can act as conductors or semiconductors. Applications include lightweight aerospace composites, high-performance electronics, and advanced energy storage systems.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Outline carbon nanotube properties (tensile strength, thermal conductivity, chirality) and applications in aerospace composites.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-019', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How sea levels are measured', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Sea level may sound like a fixed quantity, but it changes constantly with tides, waves and weather, so measuring long-term change is surprisingly difficult. The traditional instrument is the tide gauge, a device fixed to a pier or harbour wall that records the height of the water over time. Some tide gauges have records going back more than a century. Their weakness is that they measure the sea relative to the land, and the land itself can rise or sink. In some places the ground is sinking because groundwater has been pumped out, while in others it is still slowly rising after the weight of ice-age glaciers was removed. Since the early 1990s, satellites have added a second method. They send radar pulses down to the ocean surface and time how long the signal takes to return, allowing the height of the sea to be measured across almost all the world''s oceans. Combining the two methods has shown that global sea level is rising, and that the rate of rise has increased in recent decades, mainly because warmer water expands and land ice melts.', NULL, NULL, 'Sea level varies constantly, so long-term change is hard to measure. Tide gauges give records of over a century but measure the sea relative to land that may rise or sink; since the 1990s satellites have used radar to measure ocean height worldwide. Together they show sea level rising faster because of warming water and melting ice.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain why measurement is hard, the tide gauge and its weakness, satellite radar, and what the combined records show.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-020', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The paints used in Ice Age cave art', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Between thirty and forty thousand years ago, Upper Paleolithic hunter-gatherers across Eurasia began producing durable figurative paintings deep inside limestone caves. Chemical spectroscopy and micro-X-ray fluorescence analysis demonstrate that these prehistoric artists possessed sophisticated knowledge of inorganic mineral chemistry. Black pigments were derived primarily from manganese dioxide minerals and pyrolyzed wood charcoal, while vibrant red and yellow hues were prepared by grinding natural iron oxides such as hematite and goethite. Experimental archaeology indicates that artists blended mineral powders with binders like animal fat and cave water to form viscous paints applied with bone blowpipes or animal hair brushes. These stable mineral formulations bonded chemically with moist calcium carbonate cave walls, ensuring the preservation of prehistoric artistic imagery across tens of thousands of years. Pigment cakes discovered at ancient archaeological sites demonstrate that prehistoric foragers manufactured and transported standardized cosmetic and artistic recipes over extensive geographical distances. This indicates an organized system of mineral extraction and cultural trade.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture discusses the mineral chemistry of Upper Paleolithic cave paintings. Prehistoric artists processed manganese dioxide for blacks and iron oxides like hematite for reds and yellows, blending powders with animal fat binders. These mineral pigments bonded with moist limestone walls, preserving rock art for tens of thousands of years.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Detail prehistoric pigment minerals (manganese dioxide, hematite), preparation methods, and durable chemical bonding with limestone.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-021', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Where one tectonic plate sinks beneath another', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Plate tectonics explains how lithospheric plates circulate across Earth''s surface. Where a dense oceanic plate converges with a continental plate, the oceanic slab is forced downward into the mantle along a subduction zone. As the descending plate plunges deeper, increasing pressure and temperature trigger metamorphism within hydrated oceanic minerals, expelling trapped water into the overlying mantle wedge. This released fluid dramatically lowers the melting temperature of mantle peridotite through flux melting, generating buoyant magma. The ascending magma feeds chains of explosive volcanoes on the continental plate, known as volcanic arcs, exemplified by the Pacific Rim. Additionally, frictional locking along the subduction megathrust fault periodically ruptures, generating the planet''s most powerful earthquakes and catastrophic ocean tsunamis. Along active convergent boundaries, oceanic trenches mark the bathymetric surface expression where the slab plunges into the mantle. The resulting volcanic arcs contribute significantly to continental crust growth through the accretion of differentiated igneous rock masses.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture explains subduction zones where dense oceanic plates sink beneath continental crust. Released water lowers mantle melting points through flux melting, producing magma that forms explosive volcanic arcs along regions like the Pacific Rim. Locked subduction faults also generate powerful megathrust earthquakes and deadly tsunamis.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain oceanic subduction, flux melting lowering mantle melting points, volcanic arc creation, and megathrust tsunamis.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-022', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The invention of refrigeration', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Before refrigerators, people kept food cool with ice. In the nineteenth century, ice was cut from frozen lakes in winter, stored in insulated ice houses and shipped great distances, even from North America to India. Artificial cooling developed from a simple observation: when a liquid evaporates, it absorbs heat from its surroundings. In 1834 Jacob Perkins patented a machine that used this principle in a closed cycle, compressing a vapour, letting it condense, and then allowing it to evaporate again to absorb heat. This vapour-compression cycle is still the basis of most refrigerators today. Early refrigeration was used mainly in breweries, meat-packing plants and on ships carrying frozen meat. Household refrigerators became common in wealthy countries only in the twentieth century. Some early refrigerants were toxic, so they were replaced by chemicals called CFCs, which seemed safe. However, scientists later discovered that CFCs damage the ozone layer, and an international agreement in 1987 led to them being phased out. Today engineers continue to search for refrigerants that do not harm the atmosphere or contribute to climate change.', NULL, NULL, 'People once stored harvested ice, but refrigeration uses the cooling effect of evaporation; Perkins patented a vapour-compression machine in 1834, still the basis of fridges. Refrigeration spread from industry to homes in the twentieth century, and CFC refrigerants were phased out after 1987 because they damage the ozone layer.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Mention the ice trade, the evaporation principle, Perkins''s cycle, early industrial uses, household fridges and the CFC problem.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-023', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The Late Bronze Age collapse', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Around 1200 BCE, the eastern Mediterranean region experienced a widespread societal collapse that dismantled flourishing Late Bronze Age civilizations, including the Mycenaeans in Greece, the Hittites in Anatolia, and administrative centers across the Levant. Major palace complexes were burned or abandoned, written bureaucratic scripts vanished, and international trade networks disintegrated. While historical narratives frequently attributed this destruction solely to foreign invasions by enigmatic Sea Peoples, modern archaeological and paleoclimatic data indicate a multifaceted systems collapse. High-resolution lake and cave sediment cores reveal a prolonged regional mega-drought that triggered widespread agricultural failure, famine, and peasant rebellions. Interdependent palace economies, characterized by rigid centralized redistribution of goods, proved incapable of absorbing concurrent environmental shocks, internal revolts, and trade route disruptions. The widespread destruction of administrative centers stimulated the emergence of decentralized iron metallurgy, as access to imported tin and copper broke down. This technological transition marked the dawn of the subsequent Early Iron Age throughout the region.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture examines the Late Bronze Age Collapse around 1200 BCE, which destroyed Mediterranean empires. While historical accounts blamed Sea Peoples invasions, modern evidence reveals a complex systems collapse caused by prolonged drought, crop failures, earthquakes, and disrupted trade networks that overwhelmed rigid palace economies.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Examine the multi-causal systems collapse of Late Bronze Age civilizations, contrasting invasions with paleoclimate drought and trade disruption.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-024', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'From bacterial defence to gene editing', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'The discovery of the CRISPR-Cas9 system has transformed molecular biology, providing researchers with a programmable molecular tool for genome editing. CRISPR was originally discovered as an adaptive immune defense utilized by bacteria against bacteriophage viruses. When a bacterium survives a viral infection, specialized enzymes capture snippets of the invading viral genome and store them within the host DNA as spacer sequences. During subsequent infections, these spacers are transcribed into guide RNA molecules that bind to matching sequences in the invading virus. The Cas9 endonuclease enzyme then executes a double-strand break in the foreign viral DNA, neutralizing the threat. Molecular biologists repurposed this bacterial system by synthesizing artificial guide RNAs that direct Cas9 to specific genomic loci in eukaryotic cells, facilitating targeted genetic therapy for inherited human diseases. In addition to medical applications, agricultural geneticists are utilizing CRISPR to develop drought-resistant crop varieties and livestock breeds resistant to endemic viral infections. This technology provides targeted tools to bolster global food security under shifting climatic conditions.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker outlines CRISPR-Cas9, an adaptive bacterial immune defense repurposed as a revolutionary gene-editing technology. A guide RNA directs the Cas9 enzyme to cut specific target DNA sequences, enabling precise gene disruption or correction. This technique offers transformative treatments for genetic conditions like sickle cell anemia.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe CRISPR''s bacterial origin, the mechanism of guide RNA and Cas9 cutting DNA, and clinical applications.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-025', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How submarines dive and surface', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'A submarine can float on the surface or sink below it by changing its overall density, using large compartments called ballast tanks. When the submarine is on the surface, these tanks are filled with air, making the vessel light enough to float. To dive, valves are opened and the tanks fill with seawater. The submarine becomes heavier than the water it displaces, and it begins to sink. To stay at a steady depth, the crew adjusts the amount of water in smaller tanks until the submarine is neutrally buoyant, neither rising nor sinking. Small wing-like surfaces called hydroplanes are also used to steer the vessel up or down as it moves forward, much as an aircraft uses its wings. To return to the surface, compressed air stored in tanks on board is blown into the ballast tanks, forcing the water out. The submarine becomes lighter and rises. The hull must be extremely strong, because water pressure increases rapidly with depth, and the deeper a submarine is designed to go, the thicker and stronger its hull must be.', NULL, NULL, 'Submarines change density with ballast tanks: filled with air they float, filled with seawater they sink. Smaller tanks keep them neutrally buoyant and hydroplanes steer them; to surface, compressed air forces the water out. Strong hulls resist water pressure, which rises with depth.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe the ballast tanks for diving and surfacing, neutral buoyancy, the hydroplanes and the need for a strong hull.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-026', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The science of sunburn', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Sunburn is caused by ultraviolet radiation from the sun, which we cannot see or feel directly. Two types of ultraviolet light reach the Earth''s surface in significant amounts. Ultraviolet B is the main cause of sunburn: it damages the DNA in skin cells, and the redness, heat and soreness that follow are signs of the body''s response to that damage. Ultraviolet A penetrates deeper into the skin and contributes to skin ageing, and both types increase the risk of skin cancer. The skin''s natural protection is a pigment called melanin, which absorbs ultraviolet light. People with darker skin have more melanin and burn less easily, although they are not immune to damage. A suntan is actually the skin producing extra melanin after it has been exposed, so a tan is a sign of damage rather than of health. Sunscreens work by absorbing or reflecting ultraviolet radiation. The SPF number describes protection mainly against ultraviolet B. Because ultraviolet levels are strongest in the middle of the day and can be high even when it is cloudy, doctors advise using shade and clothing as well as sunscreen.', NULL, NULL, 'Sunburn is caused by ultraviolet B damaging skin cells'' DNA, while ultraviolet A ages the skin; both raise cancer risk. Melanin protects the skin, and a tan is extra melanin produced after damage. Sunscreen absorbs or reflects UV, SPF mainly measures UVB protection, and shade and clothing are also advised.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Distinguish UVB and UVA, explain melanin and what a tan means, and cover sunscreen, SPF and other protection.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-027', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How owls fly silently', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Most birds make a noticeable sound when they fly, but many owls fly almost silently. This allows them to hear small animals moving on the ground while they hunt, and to approach their prey without being heard. Scientists have identified several features of owl feathers that reduce noise. The front edge of the main flight feathers has a row of tiny stiff points, like the teeth of a comb, which break up the flow of air into smaller currents and reduce the turbulence that creates sound. The back edges of the feathers have a soft, loose fringe that further smooths the air as it leaves the wing. In addition, the surface of the feathers is covered with a velvety layer that absorbs sound when the feathers rub against each other. Owls also have large wings compared with their body weight, so they can fly slowly with fewer wingbeats. Engineers are studying these adaptations to design quieter fan blades, wind turbines and aircraft parts, which shows how research into animals can lead to practical improvements in technology.', NULL, NULL, 'Many owls fly almost silently so they can hear prey and approach unheard. Comb-like points on the feathers'' front edge break up turbulence, soft fringes smooth the air and a velvety surface absorbs sound, while large wings allow slow flight. Engineers study these features to design quieter fans, turbines and aircraft.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Give the reason for silent flight, describe the three feather features and the large wings, and mention engineering applications.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-028', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Factories that share their waste', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Traditional manufacturing systems operate along a linear model that extracts raw virgin materials, manufactures consumer goods, and discards hazardous industrial waste. In contrast, industrial ecology advocates for circular systems where the waste stream of one industrial facility serves as feedstock for another. A classic example of this operational model is the industrial symbiosis network in Kalundborg, Denmark. In Kalundborg, independent enterprises—including a power station, an oil refinery, a pharmaceutical plant, and a plasterboard factory—exchange water, energy, and material byproducts through shared pipelines. Waste steam from the power plant heats neighboring homes and powers refinery operations, while sulfur removed from exhaust gas is synthesized into industrial gypsum for wallboard manufacturing. By converting industrial byproducts into commercial inputs, this collaborative network saves millions in operating costs while dramatically reducing resource consumption. Participating enterprises in Kalundborg report that mutual trust and regulatory flexibility were essential for establishing shared infrastructure pipelines. This cooperative industrial ecosystem serves as an international benchmark for resource-efficient circular economy development.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture contrasts linear manufacturing with industrial symbiosis within a circular economy. In Kalundborg, Denmark, diverse facilities exchange byproducts through shared pipelines: power plant steam heats homes and refineries, while petroleum sulfur produces commercial gypsum. This cooperative model minimizes virgin resource consumption, lowers emissions, and cuts costs.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Contrast linear and circular economic models, using the Kalundborg eco-industrial park to illustrate byproduct exchange.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-029', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Biochar and soil', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Biochar is a fine-grained, carbon-rich charcoal produced through pyrolysis—the thermal decomposition of agricultural biomass under oxygen-deprived conditions. Inspired by ancient, fertile anthropogenic soils identified in the Amazon basin, agricultural scientists are exploring biochar for soil amendment and long-term carbon sequestration. When agricultural crop residues decay on open field surfaces, microbial decomposition returns stored carbon to the atmosphere as carbon dioxide within a few seasons. In contrast, pyrolyzing biomass stabilizes organic carbon into recalcitrant aromatic carbon rings that resist microbial enzymatic breakdown for centuries. Agronomically, biochar''s highly porous microstructure acts like a sponge, increasing soil water-holding capacity, preventing nutrient leaching from synthetic fertilizers, and providing a hospitable habitat for beneficial soil microbes, boosting long-term crop productivity. Pyrolysis also generates combustible syngas and bio-oil as valuable coproducts, which can be harvested to supply renewable process heat or electricity. This closed-loop energetic balance enhances the overall economic feasibility of rural biomass conversion facilities.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture discusses biochar, a porous carbon charcoal made by pyrolyzing agricultural biomass without oxygen. Unlike decaying plants that quickly release CO2, biochar locks carbon into stable structures for centuries. Adding biochar to soil improves moisture retention, prevents nutrient runoff, and supports microbial life, offering both climate and agricultural benefits.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain pyrolysis production of biochar, its long-term carbon stabilization compared to natural decay, and agronomic benefits.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-030', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How spiders build their webs', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Spider silk is one of the most remarkable materials in nature. It is made of proteins that are stored as a liquid inside the spider''s body and turned into solid threads as they are pulled out through organs called spinnerets. Weight for weight, some spider silk is stronger than steel, and it can stretch a long way before breaking. Many spiders can produce several different kinds of silk, each with its own purpose. A typical garden spider building an orb web first releases a thread that drifts on the wind until it sticks to a nearby branch, forming a bridge. It then builds a frame and a set of spokes from strong, dry silk. Finally, it adds a spiral of stretchy, sticky silk to catch insects. The spider knows which threads are sticky and walks on the dry ones. Many orb-weaving spiders eat their old web and rebuild it regularly, recycling much of the protein. Scientists have long wanted to produce spider silk commercially, but farming spiders is impractical because many species are territorial, so researchers are trying to produce the silk proteins using bacteria instead.', NULL, NULL, 'Spider silk is a protein pulled through spinnerets and is, weight for weight, stronger than steel. An orb spider builds a bridge line, then a frame and spokes of dry silk and a sticky catching spiral, and often eats and rebuilds its web. Farming spiders is impractical, so scientists try producing silk proteins with bacteria.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe the silk and its strength, the stages of building an orb web, recycling the web, and efforts to produce silk commercially.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-031', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'A sudden return to cold at the end of the Ice Age', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Approximately twelve thousand eight hundred years ago, as Earth was steadily warming from the Last Glacial Maximum, temperatures abruptly plunged back toward near-glacial extremes in an event known as the Younger Dryas. Over just a few decades, average temperatures across the North Atlantic region dropped by up to ten degrees Celsius, causing glaciers to readvance and ecosystems to contract for more than a millennium. The prevailing scientific hypothesis attributes this abrupt cooling to the catastrophic discharge of glacial Lake Agassiz, an immense freshwater reservoir dammed by the retreating North American ice sheet. When the ice dam failed, a massive volume of cold freshwater surged into the northern Atlantic Ocean. Because freshwater is less dense than saline seawater, it capped the surface, halting deep convection and shutting down the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation which conveys warm equatorial water northward. Geological evidence from terminal moraines confirms that European glaciers readvanced hundreds of kilometers during this cold interval. Atmospheric circulation patterns shifted globally, altering monsoon intensities across Asia and drying out tropical savannahs.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture explains the Younger Dryas event, an abrupt 1,000-year cold period interrupting post-glacial warming. The collapse of an ice dam released massive freshwater from Lake Agassiz into the North Atlantic, capping saline water and shutting down the Atlantic overturning circulation. This heat conveyor shutdown caused temperatures to plunge across the Northern Hemisphere.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Detail the Younger Dryas cooling mechanism: Lake Agassiz freshwater flood shutting down Atlantic thermohaline circulation.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-032', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Galileo and the telescope', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'The first telescopes appeared in the Netherlands in 1608, when spectacle makers discovered that combining two lenses in a tube made distant objects look closer. The following year, the Italian scientist Galileo Galilei heard about the invention and built his own, improving it until it magnified objects about twenty times. Galileo was not the first person to look at the sky with a telescope, but he was the first to study it systematically and publish his results. What he saw challenged many accepted ideas. The Moon was not a perfectly smooth sphere but had mountains and craters. Jupiter had four moons orbiting it, which showed that not everything in the sky moved around the Earth. Venus showed phases like the Moon, which was difficult to explain unless Venus travelled around the Sun. The Milky Way turned out to consist of countless individual stars. These observations supported the idea that the Earth and planets orbit the Sun, and they brought Galileo into conflict with the Church. They also showed how a new instrument could transform scientific understanding.', NULL, NULL, 'Telescopes were invented in the Netherlands in 1608, and Galileo built improved versions and studied the sky systematically. He found mountains on the Moon, four moons of Jupiter, the phases of Venus and countless stars in the Milky Way, supporting a Sun-centred system and bringing him into conflict with the Church.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Give the origin of the telescope, Galileo''s role, his four main observations and their significance.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-033', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How fireworks get their colours', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'A firework display depends on some fairly simple chemistry. A firework shell contains a charge that launches it into the air and a second charge that makes it burst, scattering small pellets called stars. The colour of each star comes from the metal compounds it contains. When these compounds are heated to a very high temperature, their atoms absorb energy and then release it as light of particular colours. Strontium compounds produce red, barium compounds produce green, and copper compounds give blue, while sodium produces a strong yellow. Blue has traditionally been the most difficult colour to create, because copper compounds lose their colour if they become too hot, so chemists must control the temperature carefully. Other effects come from different ingredients: metals such as aluminium and magnesium produce bright white sparks. The shape of a burst depends on how the stars are arranged inside the shell before it explodes. Although fireworks are enjoyable, they also release smoke and fine particles, and some towns now use drone light displays instead for large celebrations.', NULL, NULL, 'Firework colours come from metal compounds that emit light when heated: strontium gives red, barium green, copper blue and sodium yellow, while aluminium and magnesium give white sparks. Blue is hardest because copper compounds lose colour when too hot. Fireworks also cause smoke and particles, so some towns use drone displays.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain how heat produces coloured light, list the metals and their colours, why blue is difficult, and the environmental concern.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-034', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How chameleons change their skin', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Chameleons are famous for changing colour, and it is often said that they do this to match their background. In fact, colour change in many chameleons is used mainly for communication and for controlling body temperature. A male may become brighter to attract a female or to warn off a rival, while a darker colour absorbs more heat on a cool morning. For a long time, scientists believed that chameleons changed colour only by moving pigments within their skin cells. Research published in 2015 showed that another mechanism is also important. Beneath the surface, chameleon skin contains a layer of cells holding tiny crystals arranged in a regular pattern. These crystals reflect certain colours of light. When the chameleon is calm, the crystals are close together; when it becomes excited, it can adjust the spacing between them, changing which colours are reflected. This is why a chameleon can shift from green to yellow or orange so quickly. Like the colours of a soap bubble, these colours are produced by the structure of the material rather than by pigment alone.', NULL, NULL, 'Chameleons change colour mainly to communicate and control temperature, not only to hide. Besides moving pigments, research in 2015 found skin cells with tiny crystals whose spacing the chameleon adjusts, changing which colours are reflected, so colour comes from structure as well as pigment.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Correct the camouflage idea, give the real purposes, and explain the crystal mechanism discovered in 2015.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-035', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Why some people develop food allergies', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'A food allergy occurs when the immune system mistakes a harmless food protein for a threat. The first time a person is exposed, the body may produce antibodies against the protein. When the food is eaten again, these antibodies trigger the release of chemicals such as histamine, causing symptoms that range from an itchy mouth or a rash to, in severe cases, a life-threatening reaction called anaphylaxis. A small number of foods cause most allergies, including peanuts, tree nuts, milk, eggs, fish, shellfish and wheat. Food allergies appear to have become more common in many countries in recent decades, and researchers are trying to understand why. For many years, parents were advised to avoid giving young children foods such as peanuts. However, a large study published in 2015 found that introducing peanut products early, in carefully controlled amounts, greatly reduced the risk of developing a peanut allergy in high-risk children. As a result, advice has changed in several countries. People with serious allergies are often advised to carry an adrenaline injector to use in an emergency.', NULL, NULL, 'A food allergy is the immune system treating a harmless food protein as a threat, releasing chemicals like histamine and sometimes causing anaphylaxis. A few foods such as peanuts, milk and eggs cause most allergies. A 2015 study found early, controlled introduction of peanuts reduced allergy risk, changing advice, and people at risk carry adrenaline injectors.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain the immune mechanism and symptoms, the common foods, the change in advice after the 2015 study, and adrenaline injectors.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-036', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How touchscreens work', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Touchscreens are now so common that we rarely think about how they detect a finger. Most modern smartphones use a type called a capacitive touchscreen. Beneath the glass is a very fine grid of transparent conducting material, which holds a small electric charge. The human body conducts electricity, so when a finger touches the glass, it disturbs the electric field at that point. The device measures these tiny changes across the grid many times per second and calculates exactly where the touch occurred. Because it can detect several points at once, it allows gestures such as pinching to zoom. This also explains why a capacitive screen does not respond to an ordinary glove or a plastic pen, which do not conduct electricity; special gloves have conductive fingertips for this reason. An older design, the resistive touchscreen, works differently. It has two flexible layers separated by a small gap, and pressing the screen pushes them together, completing a circuit. Resistive screens work with any object, including gloves, but they are less sensitive and usually detect only one touch at a time.', NULL, NULL, 'Most phones use capacitive screens: a transparent conducting grid holds a charge, and a conductive finger changes the electric field, letting the device locate touches and detect several at once, but not through ordinary gloves. Older resistive screens work when pressure joins two layers, responding to any object but less sensitively and usually one touch at a time.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain the capacitive principle, multi-touch and the glove problem, then contrast with resistive screens.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-037', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Where domestic horses came from', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'The domestication of the horse fundamentally transformed human mobility, communication, warfare, and intercontinental trade. Archaeological discoveries from the Botai culture in modern-day Kazakhstan, dating to approximately thirty-five hundred BCE, provided early evidence of equine management, including bit-wear marks on horse teeth and pottery containing mare''s milk residues. However, recent large-scale paleogenomic investigations analyzing ancient horse DNA across Eurasia have clarified this evolutionary history. Geneticists demonstrated that modern domestic horses trace their ancestry not to the Botai lineage, which became the ancestors of wild Przewalski''s horses, but to a distinct genetic lineage that emerged in the lower Volga-Don region around twenty-two hundred BCE. Within a few centuries, this lineage spread rapidly across Eurasia, propelled by human selection for docility and a stronger skeletal backbone capable of supporting mounted riders and pulling wheeled chariots. Stable isotope analysis of human skeletal remains from the Pontic-Caspian steppe demonstrates a dietary transition following horse domestication, with mare''s milk and meat becoming pastoral staples. This dietary and mobility shift facilitated rapid demographic expansions across central Eurasia.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker discusses the domestication of the horse, contrasting Botai archaeological evidence in Kazakhstan with recent genomic data. DNA studies show that all modern domestic horses originate from the Volga-Don region around 2200 BCE rather than the Botai lineage. Selective breeding for docility and spinal strength enabled mounted riding and chariot warfare across Eurasia.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Contrast Botai culture findings with Volga-Don genomic evidence, highlighting traits selected for riding and chariots.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-038', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Earthquakes caused by human activity', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'The widespread application of horizontal drilling and hydraulic fracturing has substantially expanded natural gas extraction from shale strata. While public attention often focuses on the fracturing process itself, geophysicists emphasize that induced regional earthquakes are predominantly caused by a secondary operational step: the deep underground injection disposal of vast volumes of coproduct wastewater. After hydrocarbon extraction, billions of liters of contaminated saline water are pumped under high pressure into disposal wells miles below the surface. This injected fluid elevates pore fluid pressures along dormant pre-existing geological faults, reducing the frictional resistance holding fault walls together. When effective normal stress drops sufficiently, the faults slip, triggering induced seismic events. In response, geological regulatory agencies implement traffic-light monitoring systems that mandate volume reductions when seismic thresholds are approached, mitigating fault reactivation. Seismic monitoring networks deployed around disposal wells utilize real-time automated telemetry to detect micro-tremors before felt earthquakes occur. This enables operators to reduce injection rates proactively, successfully stabilizing subterranean pore fluid pressures.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture explains induced seismicity related to energy extraction. Geophysicists clarify that earthquakes are primarily caused by deep disposal injection of wastewater rather than the initial fracturing. High-pressure wastewater elevates pore pressure along ancient faults, reducing friction and inducing slippage, leading regulators to cap injection volumes.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Clarify that induced seismicity is caused by high-pressure wastewater disposal altering fault pore pressure, not the initial fracturing.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-039', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The first recorded voices', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'For almost all of human history, sounds disappeared the moment they were made. That changed in 1877, when Thomas Edison invented the phonograph. His machine recorded sound by making a needle cut or press a groove into a sheet of tinfoil wrapped around a rotating cylinder. When the needle was later run through the groove, it vibrated in the same pattern and reproduced the sound. The quality was poor and the foil wore out after a few playings, but people were astonished to hear a voice repeated. Within a decade, inventors had replaced tinfoil with wax cylinders, and later with flat discs, which were easier to copy in large numbers. Flat records made it possible to sell music to the public, creating a new industry. In the twentieth century, magnetic tape allowed recordings to be edited, and in the 1980s the compact disc stored sound digitally, as numbers representing thousands of measurements of the sound wave each second. Today most music is stored and streamed as digital files, but the basic idea of capturing a vibration remains the same.', NULL, NULL, 'Edison''s 1877 phonograph recorded sound as a groove in tinfoil that a needle could replay. Wax cylinders and then flat discs improved and multiplied recordings, creating a music industry; magnetic tape allowed editing, and the compact disc stored sound digitally, leading to today''s streamed files.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe the phonograph and how it worked, then trace cylinders, discs, tape, compact discs and streaming.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-040', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Water wheels in the Roman world', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'In the first century BCE, Roman architect and engineer Vitruvius documented the design of the undershot vertical water wheel in his treatise De Architectura. Prior to this innovation, grain milling relied on manual rotary querns turned by laborers or draft animals. The Vitruvian water wheel harnessed the kinetic energy of flowing river water through radial wooden paddles attached to a horizontal axle. An ingenious system of right-angle wooden gear teeth and a vertical spindle transferred this rotational motion to horizontal millstones, grinding grain at more than twenty times the speed of manual labor. Archaeological excavations at Barbegal in southern France uncovered a massive Roman milling complex consisting of sixteen cascading water wheels powered by an aqueduct, demonstrating that ancient societies utilized mechanized hydraulic engineering for large-scale industrial flour production long before the Industrial Revolution. Vitruvian water power was subsequently adapted for pumping water from deep mines using drainage wheels and Archimedean screws. These sophisticated hydraulic installations illustrate how Roman engineers applied standardized mechanical principles across diverse civil and industrial operations.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture examines ancient Roman mechanized hydropower described by Vitruvius. The vertical undershot water wheel converted flowing water into rotational force, using right-angle wooden gearing to drive millstones at twenty times manual speed. The Barbegal complex in France demonstrates that Romans deployed multi-wheel industrial mills for large-scale production.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Summarize the mechanics of the Vitruvian water wheel, right-angle gearing, and industrial flour production at the Barbegal complex.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-041', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Elisha Otis and the passenger elevator', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Lifts, or elevators, existed long before the nineteenth century, but people were reluctant to ride in them because the ropes could break. The key breakthrough was not the lift itself but a safety device. In the 1850s, an American mechanic, Elisha Otis, designed a brake that would stop the platform if the rope failed. A strong spring was attached to the top of the lift, and if the rope lost tension, the spring pushed metal teeth into ratchets on the sides of the shaft, holding the lift in place. At an exhibition in New York in 1854, Otis dramatically demonstrated his invention by standing on a raised platform and ordering the rope to be cut; the platform dropped only a short distance before stopping. The first passenger lift using his safety system was installed in a New York department store in 1857. Safe lifts changed the shape of cities. Before them, the upper floors of buildings were the least desirable, because people had to climb the stairs. Once lifts became common, tall buildings became practical, and top floors became the most valuable.', NULL, NULL, 'Otis designed a safety brake in the 1850s that locked a lift in its shaft if the rope broke, demonstrating it dramatically in New York in 1854. The first passenger lift with his system opened in 1857. Safe lifts made tall buildings practical and turned upper floors from the least into the most desirable.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain the fear of broken ropes, how the safety brake worked, the 1854 demonstration, and the effect on buildings and cities.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-042', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How dolphins and whales use echolocation', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'To navigate and forage in turbid coastal estuaries or pitch-black bathypelagic depths where sunlight cannot penetrate, toothed whales and dolphins—known as odontocetes—have evolved a sophisticated biological sonar system termed echolocation. Instead of using vocal cords, these marine mammals generate high-frequency acoustic clicks by passing pressurized air through phonic lips situated in the nasal cavity beneath the blowhole. The emitted sound clicks are focused into a directional forward beam by a specialized lipid-filled organ in the forehead known as the melon. When these acoustic waves strike submerged objects or prey fish, returning echoes travel through fat-filled channels in the lower jawbone directly to the middle ear and acoustic nerve. By interpreting the time delay, frequency shift, and amplitude of returning echoes, odontocetes reconstruct three-dimensional spatial maps of their marine surroundings with millimeter precision. Dolphins can dynamically modulate the frequency and amplitude of their biosonar clicks depending on ambient acoustic noise and target distance. This adaptive auditory control ensures reliable echolocation performance across diverse shallow coastal and deep open ocean environments.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture details echolocation in toothed whales and dolphins. Odontocetes produce ultrasonic clicks using nasal phonic lips, which are focused by the fatty melon organ into a directional acoustic beam. Returning echoes pass through the lower jawbone to the ear, allowing animals to map underwater environments and locate prey in darkness.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe odontocete biosonar: sound generation in phonic lips, melon acoustic focusing, and jawbone echo reception for navigation.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-043', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Einkorn, one of the first farmed wheats', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'The transition from nomadic foraging to sedentary agriculture during the Neolithic Revolution in the Fertile Crescent hinged on the domestication of wild cereals, particularly einkorn and emmer wheat. In wild wheat varieties, a brittle rachis allows the seed head to shatter spontaneously upon maturity, dispersing seeds across the ground. While ecologically advantageous for wild propagation, this shattering trait made grain harvesting difficult for prehistoric humans. Archaeological excavations at early Neolithic settlements such as Karacadag in southeastern Turkey reveal a gradual morphological transformation. Early farmers selectively harvested mutant plants possessing a non-shattering rachis and larger grains that remained attached to the stem. Over successive generations of selective planting and sickle harvesting, non-shattering domestic wheat became reliant on human intervention for propagation, providing the stable caloric foundation for permanent agrarian communities. Genetic mapping reveals that the non-shattering trait is governed by mutations in specific developmental genes that control abscission zone formation. Once fixed in domestic crops, these mutations rendered wheat entirely reliant on human agricultural harvesting and threshing for propagation.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker examines the domestication of einkorn wheat in the Fertile Crescent. Early farmers selected wild grasses with a non-shattering rachis mutation, preventing ripe seeds from scattering onto the ground. This artificial selection produced crops that remained on the stem for harvesting, securing food surpluses that enabled settled agricultural villages.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain cereal domestication in the Fertile Crescent, focusing on human selection for the non-shattering rachis trait.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-044', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Why we get goosebumps', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Goosebumps are the small bumps that appear on our skin when we are cold or experience a strong emotion. Each bump forms around the base of a hair. Attached to every hair follicle is a tiny muscle, and when this muscle contracts, it pulls the hair upright and pushes up the surrounding skin. The reaction is controlled automatically by the nervous system, which is why we cannot produce goosebumps simply by deciding to. In furry mammals, this response has a clear purpose. When the hairs stand up, they trap a thicker layer of air close to the skin, improving insulation against the cold. Raising the fur also makes an animal look larger, which may help to frighten away a threat, as when a cat''s fur stands on end. Humans have lost most of their body hair, so goosebumps no longer keep us warm and are probably a leftover from our ancestors. Nevertheless, the response has not disappeared, and many people experience goosebumps when listening to moving music, which shows that it is linked to emotion as well as temperature.', NULL, NULL, 'Goosebumps occur when tiny muscles at hair follicles contract, pulling hairs upright, controlled automatically by the nervous system. In furry animals this traps insulating air and makes them look bigger to threats; in humans, with little hair, it is probably a leftover, though it still happens with strong emotions such as moving music.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Describe the muscle mechanism and automatic control, the purpose in furry animals, and why it remains in humans.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-045', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The ocean''s global conveyor belt', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Global oceanic circulation is driven not only by atmospheric surface winds but also by large-scale density gradients in a worldwide system termed thermohaline circulation or the global ocean conveyor belt. In the high-latitude North Atlantic, warm surface waters transported from the tropics lose heat to the cold atmosphere. Simultaneously, the formation of sea ice expels dissolved salts into surrounding seawater through brine rejection, significantly increasing water salinity. The combination of frigid temperatures and elevated salinity produces exceptionally dense surface water that sinks rapidly into the abyss, forming North Atlantic Deep Water. This deep water flows southward along the ocean floor, traveling thousands of kilometers across the Atlantic, Indian, and Pacific basins before slowly upwelling to the surface centuries later. This global thermohaline conveyor plays an essential role in redistributing planetary thermal energy and regulating Earth''s long-term climate. Paleoceanographic sediment records demonstrate that the strength of thermohaline overturning varied dramatically between glacial and interglacial periods. Influxes of meltwater periodically disrupted deep water formation, triggering abrupt climatic shifts across the North Atlantic.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker outlines thermohaline circulation, driven by seawater temperature and salinity density gradients. In polar waters, surface cooling and brine rejection from sea ice formation produce dense, saline water that sinks to the seafloor, forming North Atlantic Deep Water. This deep conveyor redistributes heat globally across hundreds of years.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain thermohaline density drivers (temperature and brine rejection salinity), deep water formation, and global planetary heat transport.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-046', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'The invention of spectacles', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Spectacles are so ordinary today that it is easy to forget how important they have been. Before their invention, a skilled worker whose eyesight weakened with age might be unable to continue reading, writing or doing fine work. The first spectacles appeared in northern Italy around the end of the thirteenth century. They used convex lenses, which are thicker in the middle, and they helped people who had difficulty seeing things close to them, a condition that becomes common after the age of forty or so. Early spectacles had no arms; they were held in front of the eyes or balanced on the nose. Lenses for short-sighted people, which are concave, or thinner in the middle, came later. The development of printing in the fifteenth century greatly increased demand, as more people wanted to read. The arms that rest on the ears were not widely used until the eighteenth century, and in the same century bifocals, combining two kinds of lens in one frame, were invented. Spectacles effectively extended people''s working lives and contributed to the spread of reading.', NULL, NULL, 'Spectacles appeared in northern Italy in the late thirteenth century, using convex lenses for people who struggled to see close objects; concave lenses for short sight came later. Printing increased demand, arms and bifocals arrived in the eighteenth century, and glasses extended working lives and helped reading spread.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain the problem spectacles solved, the first convex lenses, later concave lenses, the effect of printing, and later design changes.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-047', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How the air bends sound', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'In atmospheric acoustics, sound waves propagating outward from a source rarely travel in straight geometric lines over long distances. Instead, variations in atmospheric temperature and wind speed with altitude cause sound waves to refract. Under normal daytime conditions, air near the ground is warmer than air aloft. Because sound travels faster in warmer air, wave fronts tilt upward, refracting sound energy away from the surface. This upward bending creates an acoustic shadow zone on the ground—an area where direct sound waves cannot penetrate, causing sound levels to drop dramatically over short distances. Conversely, during temperature inversions or when sound travels downwind, sound rays refract downward toward the ground, channeling acoustic energy and allowing sound to be heard clearly miles away. Understanding atmospheric refraction is essential for predicting environmental noise from highways, railways, and industrial facilities. Acoustic modeling software incorporates atmospheric boundary layer wind vectors and temperature lapse rates to predict sound dispersion around industrial zones. These models assist municipal authorities in placing physical noise barriers and optimizing highway buffer distances.', NULL, NULL, 'The speaker discusses atmospheric sound refraction and acoustic shadow zones. Because sound travels faster in warmer air, normal daytime temperature profiles bend sound waves upward away from the ground, creating silent shadow zones. In contrast, evening temperature inversions refract sound downward, carrying noise across much greater distances.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain sound refraction due to temperature gradients, upward bending creating acoustic shadow zones, and downward bending during inversions.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-048', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How maple syrup is made', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'Maple syrup is produced mainly in north-eastern North America, and its production depends on a particular pattern of weather. In late winter and early spring, when nights are below freezing and days are above freezing, pressure changes inside sugar maple trees cause sap to flow. Producers drill small holes in the trunks and collect the sap, traditionally in buckets and today often through networks of plastic tubes. The sap looks like water and tastes only slightly sweet, because it contains only around two per cent sugar. To make syrup, the sap is boiled for many hours to evaporate most of the water. It takes roughly forty litres of sap to make just one litre of syrup, which is one reason the product is expensive. As the sap boils, chemical reactions between sugars and other compounds create the syrup''s colour and flavour. The collecting season lasts only a few weeks, and a tree can be tapped for many decades if it is managed carefully. Because production depends on freezing nights and warm days, producers are watching closely how a changing climate affects the season.', NULL, NULL, 'Maple syrup comes from sap that flows in late winter when freezing nights alternate with warmer days. The sap, only about two per cent sugar, is boiled for hours; around forty litres make one litre of syrup, and boiling creates colour and flavour. The short season depends on weather, so producers watch climate change closely.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain the weather conditions, tapping and collection, the low sugar content and boiling ratio, flavour development and the climate concern.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-049', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'How the magnetic compass works', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'The magnetic compass is one of the most important navigational tools ever invented. It works because the Earth behaves like a giant magnet, with a magnetic field produced by the movement of molten iron deep in the planet''s outer core. A compass needle is a small, light magnet that is free to turn, so it lines up with this field and points roughly north and south. The earliest compasses were developed in China, where magnetised iron or lodestone was used by the eleventh century to find direction at sea. The technology reached Europe around the twelfth century and made it possible to navigate when the sun and stars were hidden by cloud. However, a compass does not point exactly to the geographic North Pole. It points to the magnetic north pole, which is in a different place and slowly moves over time. Navigators therefore have to correct their readings using a value called magnetic declination, which varies from one location to another. Even in the age of satellite navigation, ships and aircraft still carry compasses as a reliable backup.', NULL, NULL, 'A compass works because the Earth''s molten outer core creates a magnetic field, and a free-turning magnetised needle lines up with it. Compasses were used in China by the eleventh century and reached Europe later, allowing navigation under cloud. They point to the moving magnetic pole, so navigators correct for declination, and compasses remain a backup today.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Explain the Earth''s magnetic field and the needle, the Chinese origin and spread, the difference between magnetic and geographic north, and modern use.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
    ('pte-b3-rl-050', 'pte-2026-01', 'PTE', 'speaking', 'pte-retell-lecture', 'Re-tell Lecture', 'Einstein and the photoelectric effect', 'Listen to the lecture. After 10 seconds of preparation, retell the main points in your own words in 40 seconds.', NULL, 'In the late nineteenth century, classical wave physics predicted that illuminating a clean metal surface with light would continuously transfer electromagnetic energy to electrons until they accumulated sufficient energy to escape the metallic lattice. However, experimental observations confounded wave theory: electron emission occurred instantaneously, but only if the incident light frequency exceeded a strict threshold frequency, regardless of the beam''s intensity. In 1905, Albert Einstein resolved this dilemma by proposing that light is quantized into discrete localized energy packets, later termed photons. The energy of each photon is directly proportional to its frequency, dictated by Planck''s constant. An electron absorbs the energy of a single photon in an all-or-nothing interaction; if the photon energy exceeds the metal''s work function, the electron is instantly ejected with kinetic energy equal to the excess. This revolutionary insight established wave-particle duality and catalyzed quantum physics. The photoelectric effect provided the empirical cornerstone for quantum mechanics, proving that light exhibits both wave and particle characteristics. Today, photoelectric principles underpin photovoltaic energy generation, photodetectors, and digital image sensors in modern optical technology.', NULL, NULL, 'The lecture examines the photoelectric effect and Einstein''s explanation using quantized light. Classical wave theory failed to explain why electron ejection depended on light frequency rather than brightness. Einstein demonstrated that light consists of discrete photons whose energy is proportional to frequency, establishing wave-particle duality and founding quantum physics.', 10, 40, 15, 'medium', 'Contrast classical wave theory predictions with photoelectric observations, explain Einstein''s photon hypothesis, and work function threshold.', ARRAY['PTE', 'Original practice', 'Batch 3', 'Re-tell Lecture'], ARRAY['PTE'], TRUE),
